+## Recovery sequence fence — 2026-09-05
+
+Commit 66f6bc7bcdd091a999e62c66984fbce905cdaaed adds an observed event-sequence
+fence to failure writes from both sweep recovery and Queue exhaustion. The
+store reads the sequence, compares it under a row lock and in the update
+predicate, and asserts one changed row. The new PostgreSQL race test advances
+the event during status inspection without changing revision identities and
+proves that the stale failure cannot overwrite the newer state. Check and the
+full test suite passed; final TypeScript and six focused PostgreSQL cases
+passed. The full PostgreSQL gate was not rerun for this follow-up.
+
+The supposed shared missing-instance classifier is a type alias, not a
+production implementation. Both concrete compositions pass always-false.
+The current official local binding rewrites every status error to
+instance.not_found, so an error-message match is not accepted as absence
+proof. A verified production error contract or authenticated explicit-absence
+lookup is required. No speculative classifier or live provider query landed.
+
+The Workflow class and both Wrangler binding declarations remain pending the
+runner tranche; extend the binding-contract assertions when those land.
+Recovery status reads are bounded and rotated per tick but remain a scaling
+cost. A Queue message can reach the DLQ while the sweep later recovers its
+row; operators must inspect current PostgreSQL authority before interpreting
+that transport message as a submission failure. The adapter split, per-job
+deadlines and sole decide-and-publish writer remain the next runner work.
